<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Royal Yugoslav Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Royal Yugoslav Army was the army of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, from 1918 to 1945. Built on the Serbian army that had fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, it had a mainly Serbian officer corps. Poorly equipped and spread along long frontiers, it collapsed within days of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> invasion of April 1941 and surrendered on 17 April.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/royal-yugoslav-army.jpg" alt="Royal Yugoslav Army" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ilustrirani Slovenec · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zastava_Triglavskog_puka.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>At the end of 1918 a Serbian Army mission arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> to merge the Serbian Army with the National Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, which the National Council had formed from the Austro-Hungarian units on its territory. An agreement that Croatian and Slovene officers would command regiments in their own lands was ignored. The new army was led by Serbian generals, used Serbian as its official language and kept the Serbian Army's uniforms, ranks, medals and regulations, while former Austro-Hungarian and Croatian Home Guard officers had to apply for admission. Its early fighting was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> until September 1919, where it briefly occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klagenfurt">Klagenfurt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>By 1921 the army had 16 infantry divisions and two cavalry divisions in four army areas, with headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novi-sad">Novi Sad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a>, Skoplje and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> peacetime strength of 140,000, cut to 100,000 in 1922. A clique of politically active officers, the White Hand, was present from the start; its founder Petar Živković commanded the Guards division formed in 1925 and became prime minister under King Alexander's dictatorship in 1929. Arms came largely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>. British attachés reported tough infantry but old tactics, weak training and shortages of machine guns, radios, transport and tanks. Of 165 generals in 1938, two were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> in 1938 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> a frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and threatened its chief arms supplier. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> invaded in April 1941 the army was still mobilising, with about 600,000 men under arms by 20 March and only some eleven divisions in position. Its 1st Army Group, with the 4th and 7th Armies, was mainly Croatian, and on 10 April some of its units mutinied as a new Croatian government welcomed the Germans into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>.</p><p>After the surrender, units that escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> formed the Yugoslav Army Outside the Homeland, including the 1st Battalion, Royal Yugoslav Guards, which fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> with the 4th Indian Division and was disbanded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1944 amid feuding between royalists and supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a>. At home, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetniks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draza-mihailovic">Draža Mihailović</a> called themselves the Royal Yugoslav Army in the Fatherland. The army was formally disbanded on 7 March 1945, when the government-in-exile of Peter II was abolished.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Royal Yugoslav Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Royal Yugoslav Army</b> was built on the Serbian army that had retreated through Albania in 1915 and returned in triumph after the breakthrough on the Salonika front in September 1918, and its officer corps remained overwhelmingly Serbian, which alienated Croats and Slovenes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
